#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055} :activity/id #uuid "4918d79b-bb18-5ebc-b1d6-c9cd4c5a9bf1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c717541104571d6dce45dc88e30c4ff2dd42471d2348fe05b7d91ce395adae4.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p),\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX  \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p,$ we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}].$\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4cf166b-07f1-5e2e-9b9a-8147d42ce348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9526 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ce0e2df-ecaa-5cb0-b322-b2b78e5030ce" :step/math-academy-id 187404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189501 :tutorial/content "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n\n- $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5,$ *and*\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\n*Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right).\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5,$\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1addf4e-37b8-58a0-827e-70f370944f92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9527 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Sample Distribution Model"} :step/id #uuid "32038366-e144-50bf-8a9e-d6ae30f4a820" :step/math-academy-id 187405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188193 :question/id #uuid "ddfa9789-7019-5939-b675-bcef59e5ef4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502151}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "672cee66-8553-5cd8-8135-323a5632bbce" :step/math-academy-id 187406 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188194 :question/id #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" :question/math-academy-id "e-15152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "4c971f78-cc05-5002-90c0-b319d1ef39e2" :step/math-academy-id 187409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188195 :question/id #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" :question/math-academy-id "e-15153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"} :step/id #uuid "69641840-a7d8-502b-b5fc-162ab4534b75" :step/math-academy-id 187412}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188515}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513003} :activity/id #uuid "b137b7a5-4e68-530a-a72a-5c5c10f189b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512261} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512327} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512400} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147386" :question/problem "A fitness center has $1600$ members, $480$ of which play tennis. If the center randomly samples $60$ members, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ | $\\,0.9429\\,$ | $\\,0.9441\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ | $\\,0.9545\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ | $\\,0.9633\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{480}{1600}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & >\\frac{24}{60}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.69)=0.9545.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}) & =1-Φ(1.69) \\\\  & =0.0455.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.66)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512316} {:answer/id #uuid "873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512413} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} {:answer/id #uuid "e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.53)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab3a112-abbd-5b74-bf90-3997e193c1b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0faf39ad-2709-5ec7-94d0-99312772d48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146155" :question/problem "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.38.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.38)=76>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.38)=124>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $80$ families in the sample own a pet, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}>\\frac{80}{200}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.38}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.58) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8117" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512113} {:answer/id #uuid "1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7582" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512134} {:answer/id #uuid "27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512187} {:answer/id #uuid "473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147211" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $70%$ but less than $80%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9671\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} {:answer/id #uuid "71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.29)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512368} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512429} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512562} {:answer/id #uuid "e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} :answer-field/id #uuid "99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146974" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513021}}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512091}]}
